We need to analyze the problem.

The test scenario:

- There are three tabs: Account (id="tab-account"), Notifications (id="tab-notifications") which is disabled, Billing (id="tab-billing").

Initially, Account tab is selected (aria-selected=true, active class). Then pressing ArrowRight should move focus to the next enabled tab, skipping the disabled Notifications tab, ending at Billing tab.

Observed failure: After pressing ArrowRight from Account, the focus remains on Account? Actually the observed step shows after pressing ArrowRight, the state shows #tab-account still has aria-selected=false? Wait look at "after" block: In the after state, the HTML shows:

<ul class="nav nav-tabs mt-3" id="settings-tabs" role="tablist">
<li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link" id="tab-account" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#pane-account" type="button" role="tab" aria-controls="pane-account" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Account</button></li>
<li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link active" id="tab-notifications" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#pane-notifications" type="button" role="tab" aria-controls="pane-notifications" aria-selected="true" disabled="">Notifications</button></li>
<li class="nav-item" role="presentation"><button class="nav-link" id="tab-billing" data-bs-toggle="tab" data-bs-target="#pane-billing" type="button" role="tab" aria-controls="pane-billing" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Billing</button></li>

So after ArrowRight press, the active class moved to Notifications (which is disabled) and aria-selected true on Notifications, and tabindex=-1 on Account and Billing. So the script incorrectly moved to the disabled tab because getNextActiveElement didn't skip disabled elements? Let's examine the code.

In tab.js, the _keydown method:

```
  _keydown(event) {
    if (!([ARROW_LEFT_KEY, ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key))) {
      return
    }

    event.stopPropagation()
    event.preventDefault()
    const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)
    const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren(), event.target, isNext, true)
    Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
  }
```

It calls getNextActiveElement with isCycleAllowed = true (the fourth argument). It passes the list of children (this._getChildren()) which returns inner elements via SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM.

Now, SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM is defined as:

```
const SELECTOR_INNER = `.nav-link${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}, .list-group-item${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}, [role=\"tab\"]${NOT_SELECTOR_DROPDOWN_TOGGLE}`
const SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE = '[data-bs-toggle=\"tab\"], [data-bs-toggle=\"pill\"], [data-bs-toggle=\"list\"]' // todo:v6: could be only `tab`
const SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM = `${SELECTOR_INNER}, ${SELECTOR_DATA_TOGGLE}`
```

Thus SELECTOR_INNER_ELEM includes elements matching those selectors. For our tabs, each button has class nav-link, role tab, data-bs-toggle="tab". So they are included.

Now getNextActiveElement function is from util/index.js:

```
const getNextActiveElement = (list, activeElement, shouldGetNext, isCycleAllowed) => {
  const listLength = list.length
  let index = list.indexOf(activeElement)

  // if the element does not exist in the list return an element
  // depending on the direction and if cycle is allowed
  if (index === -1) {
    return !shouldGetNext && isCycleAllowed ? list[listLength - 1] : list[0]
  }

  index += shouldGetNext ? 1 : -1

  if (isCycleAllowed) {
    index = (index + listLength) % listLength
  }

  return list[Math.max(0, Math.min(index, listLength - 1))]
}
```

This function does not consider disabled state; it just returns the next element in the list regardless of disabled attribute. So if the next element is disabled, it will still be returned.

Thus the bug: Arrow navigation should skip disabled tabs. According to ARIA authoring practices for tablist, arrow keys should move focus to the next/previous tab, skipping disabled tabs.

Therefore we need to modify _keydown to find the next enabled tab, possibly by filtering the list to only those that are not disabled (via isDisabled function from util/index). Or we could modify getNextActiveElement to accept a filter, but better to adjust within tab.js.

Approach: In _keydown, after getting the list of children, we need to find the next enabled element in the direction, wrapping if allowed.

Simplify: We can reuse getNextActiveElement but then check if the returned element is disabled; if so, we need to keep searching.

Implementation: create a helper function that given list, current index, direction, returns next enabled element index, considering wrap.

